==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|acousticks||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|acoustic|-s}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkuː.stɪks/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-acoustics.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}
See [[-ics]] regarding the treatment of such nouns as singular.

# {{lb|en|physics}} The [[science]] of [[sound]]s, teaching their [[nature]], [[phenomenon|phenomena]] and [[law]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Herschel|year=1831|publisher=Printed for Longman, Rees, Orme, Brown, & Green: Paternoster Row, and John Taylor, Upper Gower Street|location=London|title=Preliminary Discourse on the Study of Natural Philosophy|page=248|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zSOMM44TPLIC&pg=PA248|text="'''Acoustics''', then, or the science of sound, is a very consderable branch of physics, and one which has been cultivated from the earliest ages.}}
# The properties of a space that affect how sound carries.
#: {{ux|en|The '''acoustics''' in the opera house gave the whole concert a spooky sound.}}

====Usage notes====
* The science was previously divided by some writers into [[diacoustics]], which explains the properties of sounds coming directly from (''sic!'' Webster) the ear; and [[catacoustics]], which treats of reflected sounds or echoes. This division is now obsolete.

